# Packages that go into the appliance image.
#
# The rule: a package is on this list only if something on the device actually
# runs it. No editors, no compilers, no Node, no Chromium, no desktop, no
# documentation. Everything comes from Debian trixie, trixie-backports or
# archive.raspberrypi.com - the same three sources the project already uses.
#
# Format: one package per line, "#" starts a comment. A "/suite" suffix pins
# that package to a suite the way apt does.

# --- boot: Raspberry Pi 5 ---------------------------------------------------
# Debian's own trixie kernel does not bring up a Pi 5 (raspi.debian.net says Pi 5
# needs Debian 14), so the kernel and the GPU firmware come from the Raspberry Pi
# archive - which is Debian trixie with a working kernel, not a different distro.
linux-image-rpi-2712
raspi-firmware
# vcgencmd, and nothing else from raspi-utils: the panel reads throttling and
# under-voltage from it, and no other source reports those.
raspi-utils-core
# No RTC on the board. Without this the clock starts at the epoch on every boot,
# which makes a fresh TLS certificate "not valid yet" and dates every alert 1970.
fake-hwclock

# --- system -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The build starts from minbase (essential + apt + the "required" priority), so
# everything above that is named here. What is missing is missing on purpose:
# no cron (nothing is scheduled), no ifupdown (NetworkManager does the work),
# no editor, no pager, no whiptail.
systemd-sysv
# Module loading. Not part of minbase, and without it the kernel gets no
# drivers loaded from userspace at all.
kmod
# The one diagnostic tool kept on purpose: "can this box reach the gateway" is
# the first question at every site, and the answer decides whether the problem
# is the panel or the network.
iputils-ping
systemd-timesyncd
# Keys for the three archives, so the device can still update itself.
debian-archive-keyring
raspberrypi-archive-keyring
sudo
ca-certificates
openssl
# The panel is reached at <hostname>.local. Without mDNS the operator is left
# hunting for a DHCP address.
avahi-daemon
libnss-mdns
openssh-server
# eve.json grows by megabytes an hour; the panel writes logrotate entries for it.
logrotate
# The capture helper takes a mirror port off DHCP through NetworkManager and
# switches the merging offloads off with ethtool.
network-manager
# The wireless half of it, all four needed before a card written with the
# flashing tool's Wi-Fi answer can join anything: the build installs no
# Recommends, and wpasupplicant is only recommended by network-manager. The
# firmware is the radio of the Pi itself; without it the interface does not
# exist, and the wizard's answer has nowhere to go. The database and iw are
# what keep the radio on the channels of the country it was told about.
wpasupplicant
firmware-brcm80211
wireless-regdb
iw
# USB network adapters that arrive as a CD drive with a Windows driver on it
# (Realtek RTL8153 does, often) until something tells them to switch. The
# second port of the bridge or the pair is usually such an adapter.
# deploy/rapi-usb-nic.modeswitch adds the id the data package does not know.
usb-modeswitch
usb-modeswitch-data
ethtool
# Netfilter, which is what makes blocking possible at all: NFQUEUE parks the
# packet, the engine reads it, and the verdict decides whether it continues.
# Nothing is queued until somebody turns blocking on - see deploy/rapi-ips-mode.
nftables

# First boot grows the root partition to the whole card and then the filesystem
# on it. growpart pulls in e2fsprogs (resize2fs, and the fsck.ext4 that
# `fsck.repair=yes` in cmdline.txt asks for) and fdisk (sfdisk). None of those
# were here: the image shipped an ext4 root with nothing that could grow it or
# check it, and nobody noticed because the code that calls them had never run.
cloud-guest-utils
dosfstools

# --- IDS --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8.x, not the 7.0.10 in trixie/main. Backports is a Debian archive signed with
# the same key, so "only from Debian repositories" still holds.
suricata/trixie-backports
suricata-update/trixie-backports
# 13 MB of country data for the alert map. The version in trixie/main is a
# snapshot from 2019 and does not know most of today's address pools.
geoip-database/trixie-backports

# --- panel ------------------------------------------------------------------
# From apt, not pip: the image carries no build tools and no network fetch at
# first boot. The versions are what trixie ships, which is what the code is
# tested against here.
python3
python3-fastapi
python3-uvicorn
python3-websockets
python3-psutil
